WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.112 --> 00:00:17.116
"Look what I at school today!"

2
00:00:17.476 --> 00:00:19.237
Laurie said, coming into the study.

3
00:00:19.257 --> 00:00:25.959
"Since we're celebrating the explorer Christopher Columbus on Monday, we made astrolabes."

4
00:00:27.139 --> 00:00:30.320
She proudly presented the paper astrolabe to Grandpa.

5
00:00:31.656 --> 00:00:33.318
"You did a great job!"

6
00:00:33.858 --> 00:00:35.299
Grandpa admired her work.

7
00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:38.422
"You know what these were used for, right?"

8
00:00:39.863 --> 00:00:43.486
"To measure a ship's distance from the equator," Laurie stated.

9
00:00:43.506 --> 00:00:52.314
"Correct. Sailors back in Columbus' time didn't have GPS or even a complete map of the earth!

10
00:00:53.254 --> 00:00:57.738
So they used interesting instruments to measure time and distance.

11
00:00:58.899 --> 00:01:03.687
In fact, I got something today that Columbus used on his voyage too."

12
00:01:04.868 --> 00:01:07.389
Grandpa picked up an hourglass on his desk.

13
00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:14.254
"This hourglass is similar to the ones fifteenth century Europeans used to keep time.

14
00:01:15.515 --> 00:01:22.080
Back then, one turn of the glass measured thirty minutes, but this one is a little smaller," he

15
00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:27.324
said. "A student in my Artifacts from the New World class made it."

16
00:01:28.684 --> 00:01:29.924
"What's that class about?"

17
00:01:30.325 --> 00:01:31.425
Laurie wanted to know.

18
00:01:31.445 --> 00:01:38.767
"Well, we examine artifacts that tell the story of Europeans landing in the New World.

19
00:01:39.967 --> 00:01:44.669
But I always begin with a quick history of the age of exploration."

20
00:01:44.689 --> 00:01:47.930
"Quick? How quick?"

21
00:01:48.790 --> 00:01:52.071
Laurie flipped the hourglass over and grinned at Grandpa.

22
00:01:53.251 --> 00:01:55.752
Grandpa grinned back, accepting the challenge.

23
00:01:57.452 --> 00:02:03.875
"In the 1400s trade was the fastest and easiest way to become rich.

24
00:02:05.395 --> 00:02:14.259
European explorers were discovering cities in Africa and Asia that produced spices, gold, silk, ivory, and precious gems.

25
00:02:15.639 --> 00:02:22.602
Spices like pepper and cinnamon were just being introduced to Europeans; if you could bring back a ship

26
00:02:22.662 --> 00:02:27.004
full of them, you could easily pay for the entire cost of your journey.

27
00:02:28.096 --> 00:02:32.619
The Portuguese were great shipbuilders and sailed often to Africa.

28
00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:40.403
So when Christopher Columbus thought he could sail west and find a shorter sea route to Asia, he

29
00:02:40.583 --> 00:02:43.004
asked the king of Portugal to sponsor him.

30
00:02:44.085 --> 00:02:46.606
But the king rejected Columbus' request.

31
00:02:47.066 --> 00:02:53.550
The explorer then traveled to Spain, where he asked Queen Isabella and King Ferdinand to support him.

32
00:02:54.831 --> 00:03:00.830
At first the rulers were hesitant, but the race for exploration had begun, and they didn't want to

33
00:03:00.870 --> 00:03:01.831
be left behind.

34
00:03:03.231 --> 00:03:11.696
So Isabella and Ferdinand gave Columbus three ships, ninety sailors, and lots of salted fish, biscuits, and water

35
00:03:11.756 --> 00:03:12.317
for the journey.

36
00:03:13.497 --> 00:03:16.799
The men set out on August 3, 1492.

37
00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:24.164
After two months, with still no sight of Asia, the crews nearly rebelled against Columbus and planned to

38
00:03:24.264 --> 00:03:24.644
kill him.

39
00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:32.642
Luckily for Columbus, on October 12, they landed on an island he believed was part of India.

40
00:03:33.822 --> 00:03:38.303
He named it San Salvador and called the people there Indians.

41
00:03:39.964 --> 00:03:45.565
But really, he was in the Caribbean, on an island called Guanahani.

42
00:03:46.846 --> 00:03:53.748
Its native people, the Tainos, were friendly, and the Spaniards were intrigued by their red face paint and

43
00:03:53.828 --> 00:03:54.688
gold earrings.

44
00:03:55.948 --> 00:04:03.852
The land was hot and humid, and the Tainos slept outside in hanging nets they called hammocks."

45
00:04:03.872 --> 00:04:04.913
"I love hammocks!"

46
00:04:05.373 --> 00:04:06.394
Laurie blurted out.

47
00:04:06.414 --> 00:04:10.116
"They're perfect on a summer day, aren't they?"

48
00:04:10.416 --> 00:04:11.436
Grandpa smiled.

49
00:04:11.456 --> 00:04:20.141
"The Europeans thought so too, and they brought hammocks, as well as canoes, tobacco, and other things they'd

50
00:04:20.161 --> 00:04:21.862
never seen, back to Spain.

51
00:04:23.122 --> 00:04:25.624
Columbus didn't find the gold he had hoped for.

52
00:04:26.828 --> 00:04:33.572
But he continued to claim nearby islands and govern them for Spain—even though people with their own societies

53
00:04:33.652 --> 00:04:35.553
and cultures already lived there.

54
00:04:35.573 --> 00:04:39.935
Hmph!" Grandpa shook his head before continuing.

55
00:04:39.955 --> 00:04:48.360
"His first journey was praised, but during his lifetime, Columbus never received the fame he gets today.

56
00:04:49.660 --> 00:04:56.285
Many Spanish rebelled against him, and Columbus treated the Tainos so poorly that there was no hope for

57
00:04:56.365 --> 00:04:58.286
peace between the natives and the Spanish.

58
00:04:59.287 --> 00:05:05.231
For example, Columbus turned the Tainos into slaves and forced them to find gold.

59
00:05:06.792 --> 00:05:08.693
If they didn't, he had them killed.

60
00:05:09.954 --> 00:05:17.719
Cruelty like that, along with diseases the Spanish brought from Europe, killed half the Taino population within two

61
00:05:17.799 --> 00:05:26.376
years. Disappointed in his abilities as a governor, Isabella and Ferdinand took away Columbus' privilege to govern the

62
00:05:26.416 --> 00:05:27.076
new lands.

63
00:05:28.777 --> 00:05:35.200
But Columbus Day is still a holiday in the U.S.— even though Columbus never reached North America.

64
00:05:36.361 --> 00:05:41.124
In fact, some think he wasn't even the first person to sail to the Caribbean.

65
00:05:42.464 --> 00:05:49.432
Oral histories tell of West Africans from the Mali kingdom setting out to explore the western seas, and

66
00:05:49.452 --> 00:05:51.474
of Chinese expeditions as well.

67
00:05:53.435 --> 00:05:59.819
But when Columbus reached land, he proved the existence of a land mass west of Europe, and that's

68
00:05:59.899 --> 00:06:00.900
why he's so famous."

69
00:06:00.920 --> 00:06:08.605
"Grandpa, if Columbus gets credit for finding America, how come the Americas aren't called the Columbuses?"

70
00:06:08.985 --> 00:06:10.206
Laurie asked, puzzled.

71
00:06:10.226 --> 00:06:18.992
"Well, a man named Amerigo Vespucci published a theory that the land wasn't Asia, but a new continent.

72
00:06:20.288 --> 00:06:24.889
So a mapmaker named the land 'America,' after Amerigo.

73
00:06:26.030 --> 00:06:32.291
Vespucci was a merchant and explorer who sailed to the South American coasts," Grandpa answered.

74
00:06:32.311 --> 00:06:35.732
"So who discovered North America?"

75
00:06:36.112 --> 00:06:36.932
Laurie asked.

76
00:06:38.293 --> 00:06:42.474
"It depends on what you mean by 'discovered,'" Grandpa said with a chuckle.

77
00:06:42.494 --> 00:06:49.336
"Native Americans were here for thousands of years before Europeans arrived.

78
00:06:50.636 --> 00:06:58.301
But Leif Eriksson, a Viking, is usually considered the first European to sail across the Atlantic to North

79
00:06:58.341 --> 00:07:05.345
America. His expedition arrived in what's now Canada in the year 1000.

80
00:07:06.565 --> 00:07:14.850
But since Leif didn't establish long-lasting colonies and governments, John Cabot, who sailed from England, is often considered

81
00:07:14.870 --> 00:07:17.672
the first European to claim North American land.

82
00:07:18.892 --> 00:07:21.253
He arrived in Canada in 1497."

83
00:07:21.393 --> 00:07:24.835
"Time!" Laurie called.

84
00:07:25.715 --> 00:07:27.156
The hourglass was empty.

85
00:07:27.176 --> 00:07:32.598
"I hope you're finished because I already have too much to think about.

86
00:07:34.058 --> 00:07:40.221
After hearing about the Tainos, I'm not sure if we should celebrate Columbus as a hero," Laurie admitted.

87
00:07:40.241 --> 00:07:47.004
"Yes. Sometimes history teaches you unpleasant things," Grandpa answered.

88
00:07:48.248 --> 00:07:51.709
"But knowing both the good and the bad makes you smarter.

89
00:07:52.750 --> 00:07:53.410
Do you agree?"

90
00:07:53.430 --> 00:07:56.411
"I agree," Laurie said.

91
00:07:56.431 --> 00:07:59.393
"I'm glad you're smart.

92
00:08:00.513 --> 00:08:02.234
I hope I will be too one day.

93
00:08:03.254 --> 00:08:03.954
See you at dinner?"

94
00:08:03.974 --> 00:08:06.135
"See you at dinner."

95
00:08:08.396 --> 00:08:14.639
And Grandpa watched with a smile as his granddaughter walked away with a crinkled brow, no doubt still

96
00:08:14.699 --> 00:08:17.300
questioning who really discovered America.
